<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  enough.app — hardened-runtime entitlements (docs/tauri-plan.md §4).

  Two entries, deliberately. Everything else enough does is ordinary
  unprivileged work that Developer ID distribution grants by default.

  NOT here, and why:

  * App Sandbox (com.apple.security.app-sandbox) — the app spawns uv,
    python and llama-server, and reads whatever project folder the user
    picks. Developer ID distribution does not require the sandbox, and
    adopting it would mean re-architecting all three. §4 decision.

  * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — not needed. The only
    dylibs loaded into a hardened process we sign are llama-server's own,
    which carry the same Team ID after signing. The uv-managed CPython, its
    C extensions and llama-server itself are separate PROCESSES; library
    validation does not reach across a process boundary.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory —
    WKWebView's JavaScript runs in Apple's own WebContent process, which
    carries its own entitlements. Our binary never maps executable memory.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — the bundled
    llama-server finds its dylibs through an @loader_path rpath, so nothing
    here ever sets DYLD_*. (Which is also why the dylibs live in the same
    directory as the binary: the hardened runtime strips DYLD_* anyway.)

  If a later round adds in-app installers for the extras (whisper, pandoc,
  tor, harper), revisit this file deliberately rather than by reflex.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Outbound: model weights from huggingface, python packages via uv,
         and every broker web fetch. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Inbound: the loopback listeners. uvicorn serves the UI on 127.0.0.1
         and llama-server serves inference on 127.0.0.1:8080; the WKWebView
         connects to the first and the backend to the second. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
